CAN's reliability features can also be turned against it. Every node keeps two counts, a **transmit error count** (TEC) and a **receive error count**. The Bosch spec's rules for a transmitter, simplified:

- each error while transmitting adds 8; each successful frame subtracts 1;
- at TEC 128 or more the node becomes **error passive** (it may only signal errors quietly);
- at TEC 256 or more it is **bus off**: "not allowed to have any influence on the bus", until it has seen 128 occurrences of 11 recessive bits.

These rules make a faulty node remove itself instead of jamming the car. Kyong-Tak Cho and Kang G. Shin (University of Michigan) showed at ACM CCS 2016 that a compromised ECU can cause bit errors in a chosen victim's frames on purpose, raising *the victim's* TEC until it goes bus off ([paper](https://rtcl.eecs.umich.edu/rtclweb/assets/publications/2016/ktcho_busoff_CCS_16.pdf)). They demonstrated this **bus-off attack** on a prototype bus and on two real vehicles.

