For feasibility the standard offers three approaches: attack potential, CVSS-based, and attack vector. The **attack potential** approach adds five factor scores. Simon Unger and colleagues ([arXiv 2307.14114](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.14114)) reproduce the example values from the standard's informative annex:

- elapsed time: up to 1 day 0, 1 week 1, 1 month 4, 6 months 17, more 19;
- specialist expertise: layman 0, proficient 3, expert 6, multiple experts 8;
- knowledge of the item: public 0, restricted 3, confidential 7, strictly confidential 11;
- window of opportunity: unlimited 0, easy 1, moderate 4, difficult 10;
- equipment: standard 0, specialized 4, bespoke 7, multiple bespoke 9.

A total of 0 to 13 means feasibility **high**, 14 to 19 **medium**, 20 to 24 **low**, 25 and above **very low**. A low score means an easy attack.

